WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.469
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.469 --> 00:00:07.232
Hi.

00:00:07.232 --> 00:00:08.171
&gt;&gt; Thank you for coming.

00:00:08.171 --> 00:00:08.681
It's a.

00:00:08.681 --> 00:00:09.811
&gt;&gt; It's a real honor to have you here.

00:00:09.811 --> 00:00:11.170
&gt;&gt; Great.
Thank you for having me.

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:12.680
I'm thrilled to be here.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.360
&gt;&gt; And I'm.
&gt;&gt; I've been trying to get to Stanford for

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.040
a long time so [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] We've been waiting.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.170
And congratulations too,
on the Grammy salute to music icons award.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:22.720
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.988
Mary J Blige performed a tribute song for
you.

00:00:26.988 --> 00:00:28.648
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; What was that like?

00:00:28.648 --> 00:00:29.300
&gt;&gt; My God.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.640
It was so amazing.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.840
I've known Mary for a long time, and she's
one of the people I asked to pay tribute

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:42.110
to me, and she's just such an emotional
person and she's so transparent.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:45.160
So, she started out by talking about
the fact she's going through a divorce

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:45.760
right now.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.080
Which you know,
opened everyone's heart and

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:53.060
then she talked about
what she thought of me.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.870
Which was very emotional.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:01:00.200
And yeah, it was really great,
she's an amazing person.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.830
Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So, she did no more drama if any

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:03.802
of you all know that.

00:01:03.802 --> 00:01:04.590
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:05.630
&gt;&gt; By the end of the song,

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:06.980
she was on the floor.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:11.020
So, [LAUGH] it was really quite something.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:16.310
So, there are so many parts of my position

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:21.280
that are wonderful, and
one of them is getting to know

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:26.190
several celebrities in
a very personal way.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.860
And every now and then,
I just have to pinch myself because

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.330
I think of the little girl who grew
up in Greensboro, North Carolina.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:39.110
And I had a little turntable in
my bedroom that only played 45s.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:40.470
If any of you all remember 45s.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.240
A lot of heads are nodding.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.450
They had the little spindle in the middle.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:49.870
And I grew up on The Supremes and
The Temptations.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:54.140
And they showed me what was possible,
the style and

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.540
grace of The Supremes and
how talented they were and

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:03.360
that they could go to the Copa Cabana, or
go to London and perform for the queen.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.200
So, I remember that little girl and the
fact that now in my Role at BET networks,

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.000
I get to pay tribute to people like that,
you know I know Smokey Robinson

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.143
personally I mean it just
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:16.143 --> 00:02:18.380
&gt;&gt; It just [LAUGH] even saying that gives

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:24.300
me chills, so
I'm still that fan little girl,

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.920
but I also lucky enough to have
a company where we pay tribute to.

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:37.370
African Americans, not only who can
sing and dance, and are in the arts,

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.879
but also Dr.
Keith Black who was a brain surgeon, or

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:47.560
Judith Jamison who ran Alvin Ailey,

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:52.360
or Dr. Ruth Simmons who used to be
the president of Brown University,

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:57.910
where I went, went to college, so
I'm very fortunate to be in this position.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:01.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah, given all those relationships,
being interviewed by me must be quite

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.545
the comedown.

00:03:06.545 --> 00:03:08.210
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Not at all,

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:12.800
I'm thrilled to be here with you [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:13.540
&gt;&gt; And yet,

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:16.735
I'm sure you've done your homework
much more than those people.

00:03:16.735 --> 00:03:19.250
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, we'll find out.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:21.530
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:25.470
&gt;&gt; That award was
recognition of your work.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.390
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; And contribution to the music industry,

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:30.250
your leadership in the media industry.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.600
That's where you are now.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.110
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; But if we start by

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.800
going back to your childhood, and you
talked about it briefly in North Carolina.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:44.309
How did that influence you
growing up in the 60's?

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:50.090
In the south and how did that affect
the field that you chose to pursue?

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:51.920
&gt;&gt; Well, that's a good question.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:58.310
I grew up mostly in North Carolina,
my dad was in the army as I was born in

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:03.800
South Carolina, when I was six months old
we moved to Germany then to DC then to.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:04.880
Compton, California.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.510
So, it ended up in North Carolina.

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:08.790
So, I think about that a lot.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:15.000
And it had a big impact on my growing
up and my leadership ability.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:21.740
The Greensboro was very
segregated when I moved there.

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:27.290
I went to all black Junior high school,
and I went to an all black high school,

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:32.530
and you may remember there were sit
ins at the Woolworth's in Greensboro,

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:34.560
that was after I moved there.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.500
But the town was still, I mean that was
before I moved there, I'm sorry, but

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.350
the town was still very segregated.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:46.390
We had a street down the middle of
the city called Market Street and black

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:50.990
people lived on one side of Market Street
and white people lived on the other side.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:56.490
But, that's sounds horrible, but
we never felt like we lack for anything.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.970
We have black doctors and black lawyers.

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:01.440
There's a black pink.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.210
And we could shop on market street.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:09.080
So, we felt like we had
our own community and

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:12.290
one thing it showed me that you
could be anything you wanted to be.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:15.860
We had teachers who cared
about us who really pushed us

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:19.580
who made sure that we had
the basic skills that we needed.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:24.360
And they told us every day you
can be anything you want to be.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:29.270
The second part of it was the first day
I was in sixth grade in Greensboro,

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.660
they elected me president of the class.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:38.030
And it was because I was that weird
girl from LA [LAUGH] just moved to town.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:42.890
And I was very shy child,
but after they elected

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:48.130
me President of the class I had to kind of
step up and show some leadership ability.

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:55.260
And I think that was just the start of
Developing a sense of self-confidence and

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:59.180
a sense of caring about my neighborhood.

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:01.320
And about where I came from.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:05.850
And about the people that I lived with and
my family.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:09.840
And having that sense of self and

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.510
that self confidence, and
those leadership abilities and

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:20.190
the basic skills that our teachers made
sure we had, I was able to go from

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:25.560
an all black public high school to Brown
University in Providence, Rhode Island.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.039
And that was an amazing Step for

00:06:30.039 --> 00:06:34.650
me, but
I felt very confident in doing that.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:41.460
And to answer your question specifically,
I think growing up in that environment,

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:45.420
loving brands that represented
the black community,

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:49.921
like Mo Town, and Ebony, and.

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:53.110
Allowed me to

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:58.980
be excited about the opportunity
of working at a company like BET.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:03.970
When I joined BET 30 years ago,
it was still black owned.

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:07.910
It would have been started by
Bob Johnson who is our founder.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:13.770
And I was at a point, I'm skipping
over a lot here so I apologize.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:19.826
There is law school and a law firm and
clerkship in between [CROSSTALK] Yeah.

00:07:19.826 --> 00:07:21.153
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:21.153 --> 00:07:23.195
&gt;&gt; But to skip ahead to BET,

00:07:23.195 --> 00:07:29.630
when I made the decision to become general
counsel at BET, it was so exciting.

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:33.170
because I could see the promise
of this company, and

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:36.820
the fact that it provided
black entertainment.

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:41.560
And the fact that we still didn't
see enough images of ourselves.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:42.150
On TV.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:45.740
I mean I grew up in
the three network days,

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:49.220
where it was just ABC,
I know you're all going to really laugh,

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:54.080
because this is so unbelievable to
anyone who's under probably 50.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:57.810
That there were three networks,
ABC, CBS, and NBC.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.140
I mean, I remember when Fox started and

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:03.000
everyone was like,
that fourth network will never make it.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.232
Right?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:08:04.232 --> 00:08:07.674
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Why would we ever need a fourth

00:08:07.674 --> 00:08:08.382
network?

00:08:08.382 --> 00:08:14.760
But so the images of African Americans
on TV just didn't exist,

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:19.190
we would really call each other when
the Supremes were on Ed Sullivan, or

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:23.820
when a black person was,
when Julia had her,

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:27.440
Dianne Carrol had her
first show on network TV.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.730
There are a lot of heads nodding,
so you all remember.

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:35.890
So that was big news, so to be able
to work at BET, a network that for

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:41.490
24 hours a day focused on African
Americans was really very exciting for me.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:46.472
So now, as I think about it in retrospect,
I see there's a direct

00:08:46.472 --> 00:08:52.091
relationship between how I grew up and
why I'm so passionate about BET and

00:08:52.091 --> 00:08:57.434
what we do, and why I've been able
to stay at this company 30 years,

00:08:57.434 --> 00:09:03.266
and I started as general counsel and
worked my way over to the business side.

00:09:03.266 --> 00:09:08.673
But it's been a labor of love and
such a passion of mine and

00:09:08.673 --> 00:09:14.216
I still get really excited about
what we do from day to day.

00:09:14.216 --> 00:09:17.690
&gt;&gt; And focusing in on that first
transition as you said you were

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:22.260
at Brown University then Harvard
Kennedy School, Harvard Law school,

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:25.651
working in DC as an attorney for
five years.

00:09:25.651 --> 00:09:27.965
You were every parent's dream child.

00:09:27.965 --> 00:09:29.608
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I wouldn't go that far,
but okay.

00:09:29.608 --> 00:09:32.464
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My parents' dream child.

00:09:32.464 --> 00:09:33.125
&gt;&gt; Yeah, okay.

00:09:33.125 --> 00:09:39.355
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's for me to work out.

00:09:39.355 --> 00:09:41.720
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's hilarious.

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:46.515
&gt;&gt; You then made the transition to
joining a young startup called BET.

00:09:46.515 --> 00:09:49.970
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; A lot of us here are at a similar stage

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:50.580
in life.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:54.233
Particularly our joint degree law school-
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:09:54.233 --> 00:09:56.257
&gt;&gt; Classmates like Cara.

00:09:56.257 --> 00:09:57.474
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:09:57.474 --> 00:09:58.473
&gt;&gt; I just got an evil look.

00:09:58.473 --> 00:09:59.089
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:09:59.089 --> 00:10:01.155
[LAUGH] Who happens to be my cousin.

00:10:01.155 --> 00:10:02.736
I've known her for a long time [LAUGH].

00:10:02.736 --> 00:10:03.676
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:10:03.676 --> 00:10:06.000
How did you think about that decision?

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.330
&gt;&gt; Well it was a tough decision.

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:09.590
I'll back up a little bit.

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:11.500
I never really wanted to go to law school.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.940
I was one of those people
who went to law school,

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:17.348
because I had no other talents [LAUGH].

00:10:17.348 --> 00:10:20.541
It's either I go to law school or I go to-
&gt;&gt; You just got an evil look.

00:10:20.541 --> 00:10:24.083
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well it's true I was either,

00:10:24.083 --> 00:10:28.403
when I went to Brown at freshman week it
was clear, you either went to law school

00:10:28.403 --> 00:10:31.620
or you went to medical school
if you wanted to be a success.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:34.390
Even business school wasn't
that popular at the time.

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:40.110
They existed but it wasn't, you know as
popular as law school or medical school.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:43.760
So I wasn't good at sciences, so
I was like okay it will be law school, but

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:49.500
I was that student who went to law school
in order to use law for something else.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:52.220
I never really wanted to practice law, and

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:58.160
then when I landed at Harvard Law School,
and I just have to divert for

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:01.480
just a minute, because I really have
been trying to Stanford for a long time,

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:06.389
[LAUGH] and Standford was the only
school that I applied, to Stanford Law.

00:11:06.389 --> 00:11:10.600
The only other school I would have
gone to, other than Harvard, and

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:14.450
I didn't really want to go to Harvard,
because I had already been in New England

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:18.760
for four years at Brown, but
Stanford put me on the waitlist.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:22.719
So if you know anyone-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGHS].

00:11:22.719 --> 00:11:27.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] In the admission's office at
the Law School at Stanford that's why I

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:32.110
didn't come here and I was [LAUGH],
I really would have, I would have declined

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:36.750
Harvard in a minute and come to Stanford,
but Stanford put me in the waitlist.

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:38.610
I was on my way to Brown,

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:43.460
I mean sorry on my way to Harvard driving
up with a friend who was going there too,

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:48.440
and I got the notification that Stanford
had accepted me, and it was too late,

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:52.640
I'd already paid my room deposit and
I remember how disappointed I was.

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:53.180
But anyway,

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:56.571
that's just a side story so [LAUGH]-
&gt;&gt; We can bring that up

00:11:56.571 --> 00:11:57.392
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; And

00:11:57.392 --> 00:12:01.940
I may have been a happier person if
I had gone to Stanford, I don't know.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:05.659
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Obviously some people think I

00:12:05.659 --> 00:12:06.745
would have.

00:12:06.745 --> 00:12:10.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, I went to Harvard.

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:16.156
You know, again, against my wishes, but
it was the best law school I got into,

00:12:16.156 --> 00:12:18.219
and so, I went-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:12:18.219 --> 00:12:20.459
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And I didn't mention, but

00:12:20.459 --> 00:12:22.144
I had boyfriend at UCLA.

00:12:22.144 --> 00:12:23.809
That was part of that
California attraction, too.

00:12:23.809 --> 00:12:25.108
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, here we go.

00:12:25.108 --> 00:12:25.907
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry, it was part of it, yeah.

00:12:25.907 --> 00:12:28.163
&gt;&gt; It's now becoming clear.

00:12:28.163 --> 00:12:33.370
&gt;&gt; But anyway, and when I get to Harvard,
I really realized I hated law school.

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:35.570
I mean there's nothing about it I liked.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:42.725
They didn't support students who
are interested in public policy,

00:12:42.725 --> 00:12:44.535
which is what I was interested in.

00:12:44.535 --> 00:12:46.675
The school didn't support women.

00:12:46.675 --> 00:12:50.555
The school didn't support
African Americans or minorities.

00:12:50.555 --> 00:12:58.495
This was in the late 70s, and Harvard was
still very black letter law, by the book.

00:12:58.495 --> 00:13:02.135
If a court didn't decide it,
we're not going to talk about it.

00:13:02.135 --> 00:13:05.060
We don't care whether it's good law or
bad law.

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.430
So that, to me, was very difficult.

00:13:07.430 --> 00:13:09.390
So that's when I applied
to the Kennedy School.

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:11.260
I was like, okay, this is my way out.

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:15.360
I'll go to Kennedy School, I can talk
about public policy all day long.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:18.750
So I got the joint degree,
I got a masters in public policy.

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:21.418
So my plan was to go to Washington,

00:13:21.418 --> 00:13:26.350
and go into,
become assistant secretary of something.

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:30.878
I just wanted to make policy and
help my community.

00:13:30.878 --> 00:13:35.510
I originally wanted to go to law school
because of Thurgood Marshall and

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:40.070
the people who have changed
laws to help society, but

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:44.030
by the time I got to law school,
those days were kind of over, and

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:46.890
it was all about corporate law and
black letter laws.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:51.884
So, I thought the Kennedy school
was going to give me that out,

00:13:51.884 --> 00:13:56.221
but then I got to Washington and
Ronald Reagan won, and

00:13:56.221 --> 00:14:00.368
I didn't want to go into
a Reagan Administration, so

00:14:00.368 --> 00:14:03.690
that's the long story of how I got to BET.

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:05.259
But, what was the question again?

00:14:05.259 --> 00:14:10.238
[LAUGH] I'm sorry, I got so involved.

00:14:10.238 --> 00:14:13.502
&gt;&gt; I think the answer was more
interesting than the question.

00:14:13.502 --> 00:14:17.627
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It was looking at that transition,

00:14:17.627 --> 00:14:22.240
so making the jump from working as
an attorney into this new start up.

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:26.680
&gt;&gt; Right, so I ended up, yeah,
I have to finish the life story.

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:31.189
So I ended up going to a law firm to
hide out til the democrats came back.

00:14:31.189 --> 00:14:33.279
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:14:33.279 --> 00:14:33.815
&gt;&gt; So, but

00:14:33.815 --> 00:14:37.420
it took 12 years for
the democrats to come back, and so

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:41.023
after five years at a law firm,
I had to make a decision,

00:14:41.023 --> 00:14:46.011
do I really want to buckle down and kill
myself for two years to become partner,

00:14:46.011 --> 00:14:51.320
which is something I never really wanted
to do anyway, or is it time to leave?

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:53.220
So I decided it was time to leave.

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:56.280
So I started interviewing around, and

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:59.460
by that time I was doing
mostly communications.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:01.720
FCC regulatory work, so

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:07.190
I decided I wanted to go in-house
with a communications company.

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:09.280
So I interviewed mostly in New York, and

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:13.500
I wasn't sure I wanted to live in New York
and luckily BET was a small client,

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:20.110
and the founder asked me to come and
start the legal department.

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:25.705
So, I went from a 200 person law
firm called Steptoe &amp; Johnson,

00:15:25.705 --> 00:15:30.150
where I was one of 200 lawyers,
to a 80 person

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:36.120
company where I was the only lawyer.

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:41.960
So that was a big transition, and I
remembered telling my father, back to your

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:48.310
point about being the ideal child,
up to then, I kind of was.

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:50.670
I went to Harvard Law School,
they were all very proud.

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:53.120
I went to this big corporate law firm.

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:56.438
That someone like my father
didn't even know existed.

00:15:56.438 --> 00:16:01.870
He's so come and visit me and he was full
of pride because we had chandeliers and

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:05.230
antique furniture and
it's a corporate law firm.

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:09.280
But for someone like him, I thought
he spent his life in the military.

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:11.330
He didn't know places like this existed,
so

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:15.055
he just knew I had good pension benefits.

00:16:15.055 --> 00:16:18.400
[LAUGH] It's going all work out for
his daughter, so

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:23.840
then one day I say by the way I'm
leaving this really nice law firm.

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:28.200
And I'm going to a company called
Black Entertainment Television

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:30.560
which you have never
heard of before in life.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:33.100
Because at the time BET,

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:38.880
we didn't have cable in DC,
I actually worked on the cable franchise.

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:43.800
So no one in DC knew,
no one in most of the major cities,

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:48.560
because major cities were just starting
to get cable, had ever heard of BET.

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:53.080
So my father looked at me, and
he said, why would you leave [LAUGH]

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:57.190
this very comfortable 200
person law firm [LAUGH] and

00:16:57.190 --> 00:16:59.940
go to company that no
one has ever heard of?

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:04.220
And I said,
well that I'm not just having fun anymore.

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:07.355
And I said,
I just think work should be fun.

00:17:07.355 --> 00:17:11.412
And he said, well if this will suppose
to be fun, they wouldn't call it work.

00:17:11.412 --> 00:17:12.624
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:17:12.624 --> 00:17:13.970
&gt;&gt; And certainly not the law firm.

00:17:13.970 --> 00:17:20.620
&gt;&gt; Right, right, and this is a man
who had spent 24 years in the army.

00:17:20.620 --> 00:17:24.570
I'm sure he never had fun one
day of his working career.

00:17:24.570 --> 00:17:30.298
But he was, making a life for his family
and security and everything else.

00:17:30.298 --> 00:17:38.100
So my father was a Major in the army, so
it's really hard disagreeing with him.

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:41.260
You took your life in your
hand if you disagree with him.

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:44.998
So, which is why I didn't tell them
I have applied to Harvard Law School,

00:17:44.998 --> 00:17:47.004
because I didn't want
him make me go there.

00:17:47.004 --> 00:17:50.380
So I made a decision on my own, I remember
the day I told him I was going to Harvard,

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:52.540
he's like,
well you didn't even applied there.

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:55.169
And I said,
well I just didn't told you that I apply.

00:17:55.169 --> 00:17:55.828
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:17:55.828 --> 00:17:57.961
I came to the right decision on my own.

00:17:57.961 --> 00:18:02.120
But anyway, so he was a little
disappointed in me for a minute, but

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:06.906
then once I got to BET and he started
sending me, he would clip out articles.

00:18:06.906 --> 00:18:13.040
It was before your time,
before the email and internet and Twitter.

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:17.290
If you got a newspaper article that
you liked, you would clip it out and

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:18.960
send it in the mail to someone [LAUGH].

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:22.635
That's how we shared things,
and so my dad.

00:18:22.635 --> 00:18:25.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] It sounds so archaic.

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:28.940
&gt;&gt; You tell them in your Twitter
board meeting about that.

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:29.920
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah right,
that's how we shared.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:33.440
So my father lived in Baltimore
when I lived in DC, and

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:37.310
he would cut out articles, and put them
in an envelope, and mail them to me.

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:42.540
And he started seeing articles about BET,
and he passed away in 1998,

00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:49.220
but before he passed away, I had become
COO, and he became proud of me again.

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:52.180
So that was great, I was happy,

00:18:52.180 --> 00:18:56.440
but you know his first reaction
was why would you do this?

00:18:56.440 --> 00:18:58.190
And to be honest,

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:03.460
it was a first time in my career I felt
like I was stepping off of the fast track.

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:07.380
And doing something that I
wanted to do because I wanted to

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:11.840
do it as opposed to continuing
to build my resume.

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:15.610
I clerked because the clerkship fell in
my lap, I didn't really want to clerk.

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:19.570
But at the last minute
a judge I had worked for

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:24.240
at Harvard knew someone in DC who
needed a clerk, so I got the clerk.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:28.860
Clerkships I did that, and
it looked good on my resume and

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:35.750
I went to the law firm and because I was
waiting for the Democrats to come back.

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:40.190
And I knew it would look good on
my resume, so when I went to BET,

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:44.150
it was the first time I said,
this is something I really want to do.

00:19:44.150 --> 00:19:47.700
It's not something I'm doing
just to build my resume.

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:51.380
And you'll find, those of you
who are starting your careers.

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:54.141
You'll find that at a certain
point in your life,

00:19:54.141 --> 00:19:57.707
you get to that point where it's like,
this is what I want to do.

00:19:57.707 --> 00:20:02.376
I have enough qualifications,
I've done all the right things, and

00:20:02.376 --> 00:20:06.375
now it's time to really devote
myself to what I want to do.

00:20:06.375 --> 00:20:08.665
For some people it comes
early in your career,

00:20:08.665 --> 00:20:12.535
I was lucky it came relatively
early in my career.

00:20:12.535 --> 00:20:17.150
For some people,
they don't come, so later on.

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:21.240
Some people not until they retire and
they made enough money,

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:23.530
and can really do what they want.

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:28.640
But the job at BET was the first job I
accepted that I was excited about, that

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:33.420
I really wanted to do, and I didn't think
about what was going to be the next step.

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:37.200
I could see myself being at
the company for a long time, and

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:41.240
it's been 30 years so
it's kind of worked out.

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:45.210
&gt;&gt; And coming back to something that you
said, you alluded to the importance of

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:49.110
media in educating viewers
about the groups of people.

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:53.400
People with different religions,
different sexualities, essentially and

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:57.190
by inference BET are all on
behalf of the Black community.

00:20:58.280 --> 00:21:02.730
How do you think about conscious
programming particularly given that BET

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:07.440
has received some criticisms for
re enforcing negative stereotypes?

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:10.650
&gt;&gt; Well, we haven't received that
criticism in a long time and

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:11.940
I'm happy to say.

00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:14.890
Early on, BET received

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:19.040
quite a bit of criticism because we
were primarily a music video channel.

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:22.990
And when we started,
music videos were Lionel Richie,

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:25.850
and Aretha Franklin, and
Earth, Wind and Fire.

00:21:25.850 --> 00:21:29.560
Then all of the sudden something
called hip hop came along, and

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:32.840
the nature of music videos changed.

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:39.120
And, people, artists were criticized for

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:47.060
lyrics that were misogynistic and
hateful in some way.

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:51.730
And at that point,
60% of our programming was music videos.

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:55.590
So when I took over as
CEO about 11 years ago,

00:21:55.590 --> 00:22:02.060
I made a promise to do
more original programming.

00:22:02.060 --> 00:22:06.630
And that was about the time other
cable networks were getting into more

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:08.350
original programming.

00:22:08.350 --> 00:22:13.410
And we actually, my leadership team and I,

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:19.490
kind of sat down for 18 months and
said, what do we want to stand for?

00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:20.710
What does BET mean?

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:26.250
We can no longer say that we're the only
black network that we were staring to have

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:27.370
competitors.

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:32.580
But we had to come up with our brand
promise and our set of values.

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:38.220
And the three pillars of our
value promise was to respect,

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:41.740
reflect and elevate our audience.

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:46.360
And if programming didn't meet at
least two of those three pillars,

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:47.930
then we shouldn't do it.

00:22:47.930 --> 00:22:53.627
So that's when we really started
getting into more original programming,

00:22:53.627 --> 00:22:57.465
like The Game, and
Being Mary Jane, and The Quad.

00:22:57.465 --> 00:23:01.878
And we didn't want to label
programming as positive or

00:23:01.878 --> 00:23:05.220
negative because that would be misleading.

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:09.840
But what we said is we wanted
to have realistic images.

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:13.230
And if someone in a series
has a drug problem,

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:16.020
let's try to find a resolution for them.

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:20.370
Let's not sugar coat it and
pretend like it don't exist.

00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:27.220
Let's show a resolution,
let's have a storyline that

00:23:27.220 --> 00:23:33.020
really inspires people and motivates them.

00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:38.830
So I think our audience's view as
to who we are has really changed,

00:23:38.830 --> 00:23:42.970
especially in the past five or
six years, and I'm really proud of that.

00:23:44.550 --> 00:23:51.010
When we did this brand analysis,
I hired the guy named Stan Slap.

00:23:51.010 --> 00:23:53.441
&gt;&gt; Who I met at Microsoft.

00:23:53.441 --> 00:23:59.715
Microsoft has a CEO conference every year
and Stan did a seminar on brands, and

00:23:59.715 --> 00:24:04.847
he was a consultant for Microsoft,
and did a lot of brand work for

00:24:04.847 --> 00:24:10.100
them, and I went to his seminar,
and I was so impressed with him.

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:11.183
I said, please come help us.

00:24:11.183 --> 00:24:13.013
And so I hired him and

00:24:13.013 --> 00:24:18.950
he actually did This program
which as I said, took 18 months.

00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:22.506
And as he described it,
we started in hell.

00:24:22.506 --> 00:24:28.459
And hopefully, we ended up in heaven
[LAUGH] by the end of the 18 months and

00:24:28.459 --> 00:24:31.010
you'll be happy to know.

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:34.718
In heaven, there's lots of chocolate and

00:24:34.718 --> 00:24:40.804
[LAUGH] it's a really nice conference
room, but hell was hard to take.

00:24:40.804 --> 00:24:47.930
And hell consisted of interviewing our
employees, interviewing our viewers.

00:24:47.930 --> 00:24:52.460
Talking to our cable operators,
our advertisers and

00:24:52.460 --> 00:24:57.420
really getting their viewpoint of what we
were doing and where we were and the first

00:24:57.420 --> 00:25:01.700
problem we found is our employees were
not proud with what we were doing.

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:05.588
They were getting criticism from their
families and friends as why does

00:25:05.588 --> 00:25:09.294
BET show these music videos, and
why don't you do more about this?

00:25:09.294 --> 00:25:15.720
And at particular time, I had protesters
outside my house in Washington.

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:21.784
This minister and only Maryland decided,
he didn't like hip hop music and

00:25:21.784 --> 00:25:27.654
he decided the way to change it was to
buses congregation to my house every

00:25:27.654 --> 00:25:33.060
Saturday afternoon and they had
bull horns and we are not bitches.

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:34.553
We are not whores.

00:25:34.553 --> 00:25:38.994
It was not the kind of thing you want in
your neighborhood on Saturday afternoon

00:25:38.994 --> 00:25:41.810
when you're usually trying to take a nap.

00:25:41.810 --> 00:25:46.015
And so that got us a lot of attention and
I always said,

00:25:46.015 --> 00:25:52.102
why didn't he go to Jay-Z's house or
Ludacris's house or Kanye's house?

00:25:52.102 --> 00:25:54.726
[LAUGH] But
I guess they live too far away.

00:25:54.726 --> 00:25:56.121
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:25:56.121 --> 00:26:00.178
he decided that my house
was a nice place to come.

00:26:00.178 --> 00:26:04.607
So that was in the midst of
what we were going through and

00:26:04.607 --> 00:26:09.516
it took a lot to get us to heaven,
and heaven was really a place

00:26:09.516 --> 00:26:14.940
where we could see the kind of
original programming we wanted to.

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:17.670
He took all constraints off
of our resources, of course,

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:19.745
that doesn't exist in
the day to day world.

00:26:19.745 --> 00:26:24.008
But we could say, okay, what could
we do if we had unlimited resources?

00:26:24.008 --> 00:26:33.070
And I remember him saying that Apple was
a brand that we should try to emulate.

00:26:33.070 --> 00:26:37.734
Because if Apple was ever
attacked in the media or

00:26:37.734 --> 00:26:42.625
in the news,
the users of Apple products would come

00:26:42.625 --> 00:26:46.852
to their defense quicker than anyone else.

00:26:46.852 --> 00:26:49.257
Apple never had to defend itself.

00:26:49.257 --> 00:26:53.290
It always the people who had Apple
products and this was years ago.

00:26:53.290 --> 00:26:59.334
So it wasn't everyone at that point,
[LAUGH] but people who used iPhones or

00:26:59.334 --> 00:27:05.182
iPads were such true believers in
Apple that they would get together and

00:27:05.182 --> 00:27:08.397
defend Apple and I say that to say we just

00:27:08.397 --> 00:27:13.172
had a recent incident with a show
we have called The Quad and

00:27:13.172 --> 00:27:17.880
The Quad is about a HBCU campus and
it's a scripted show.

00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:22.875
It's fiction and
it's about a female president of

00:27:22.875 --> 00:27:27.064
a university, and she's a little messy.

00:27:27.064 --> 00:27:32.189
She has some issues in her life,
if any of you have watched it and

00:27:32.189 --> 00:27:35.980
the band leader is not a great person.

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:39.746
There are good people and
bad people like are in any drama, but

00:27:39.746 --> 00:27:42.796
we were one of the HBCU
presidents wrote a letter.

00:27:42.796 --> 00:27:46.868
And said, he didn't approve of it,
of the show and

00:27:46.868 --> 00:27:51.417
he thought we were giving off
negative images of HBCUs and

00:27:51.417 --> 00:27:56.911
I talked to him and assured him that,
that wasn't what was going on,

00:27:56.911 --> 00:28:03.400
that it was just where it was located and
that we all were supportive of HBCUs.

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:05.084
But anyway, long story short.

00:28:05.084 --> 00:28:09.993
The great thing was that our viewers
came to our support and said,

00:28:09.993 --> 00:28:11.964
look, this is a fiction.

00:28:11.964 --> 00:28:14.133
We all realize it's fiction.

00:28:14.133 --> 00:28:15.544
It's a great show.

00:28:15.544 --> 00:28:21.374
It's dealing with important issues like
drugs on campus and sexual harassment and

00:28:21.374 --> 00:28:26.037
abuse on campus and we're looking for
resolution on the show, but

00:28:26.037 --> 00:28:30.420
the part that really resonated with me and
made me feel good.

00:28:30.420 --> 00:28:35.095
I called my head of marketing and PR and
I said, think we've gotten to heaven.

00:28:35.095 --> 00:28:40.620
I think [LAUGH] for the first time,
we don't have to defend ourselves.

00:28:40.620 --> 00:28:45.970
People are coming to our defense and our
viewers are coming to our defense, and I

00:28:45.970 --> 00:28:51.489
think that was recognition by our audience
that we have been doing good work.

00:28:53.130 --> 00:28:58.120
Everything from the Love and Happiness
concert we did for president and Mrs.

00:28:58.120 --> 00:29:04.190
Obama or news specials we do, or
the scripted shows we do, or.

00:29:04.190 --> 00:29:09.462
So it took a while and it took a lot
of work, and a lot of new shows,

00:29:09.462 --> 00:29:13.890
and you can really tell people
what you're going to do.

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:15.185
You have to show them,

00:29:15.185 --> 00:29:19.478
but I think we're at the point now where
our audience really supports us and

00:29:19.478 --> 00:29:24.420
believes us in what we're trying to do and
gives us a little bit of leeway.

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:27.530
I never said,
we wanted to be the black PBS.

00:29:27.530 --> 00:29:29.275
That's not what we're trying to be.

00:29:29.275 --> 00:29:33.470
We want to be a network and
treat it like ABC who does Scandal, but

00:29:33.470 --> 00:29:37.920
no one says, they're ruining
the reputation of the White House.

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:41.407
People realize it's a show, it's fiction.

00:29:41.407 --> 00:29:42.902
How to get away with murder.

00:29:42.902 --> 00:29:46.750
No one says, they're ruining
the reputation of law professors.

00:29:46.750 --> 00:29:51.528
[LAUGH] I mean, because Viola Davis
is more than a little messy.

00:29:51.528 --> 00:29:54.653
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So all I wanted was for

00:29:54.653 --> 00:30:00.940
us to be treated like that, that no one's
going to like every show you put on.

00:30:00.940 --> 00:30:04.202
No one's going to like every
character you put on, but

00:30:04.202 --> 00:30:07.774
treat us like a network that
has good shows and bad shows.

00:30:07.774 --> 00:30:10.856
And if we put on a bad show and
no one watches it,

00:30:10.856 --> 00:30:13.820
we take it off like any other network.

00:30:13.820 --> 00:30:19.280
We get a daily report card called ratings,
that's telling us what people like and

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:26.860
what they don't like and it's not a
science in terms of creating programming.

00:30:26.860 --> 00:30:29.470
Things that you think are going to work,
don't work and

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:31.162
that's just part of the process.

00:30:31.162 --> 00:30:36.899
So I think we've come a long way in
showing our audience who we are and

00:30:36.899 --> 00:30:41.240
what we believe in, and
increasing our audience.

00:30:41.240 --> 00:30:45.085
I mean, I don't know how many of you saw
when we did this mini series not too long

00:30:45.085 --> 00:30:47.602
ago on new edition and
we got 29 million viewers.

00:30:47.602 --> 00:30:51.915
So that wasn't all black viewers,
that was a mix of viewers.

00:30:51.915 --> 00:30:55.301
That were viewers who watched
the show three or four times,

00:30:55.301 --> 00:30:57.340
because it was a high quality show.

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:05.485
So, I think we're at a very different
place than we were 10 or 15 years ago.

00:31:05.485 --> 00:31:06.443
&gt;&gt; I understand.

00:31:06.443 --> 00:31:10.038
Looking now at one of things
at the view from the top,

00:31:10.038 --> 00:31:13.350
we focus on is the idea of leadership.

00:31:13.350 --> 00:31:17.648
If I have a spectrum on one end,
it's a more introverted,

00:31:17.648 --> 00:31:22.498
more reserved than the other
more extroverted, more outgoing.

00:31:22.498 --> 00:31:26.324
Where would you place your
self on that spectrum?

00:31:26.324 --> 00:31:29.628
&gt;&gt; That's a great question and
that's something

00:31:29.628 --> 00:31:34.500
that I've come to understand more
the longer I've been a leader, and

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:37.900
the longer I've been CEO,
and COO before then.

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:43.313
I would say,
I have moved along the continuum and

00:31:43.313 --> 00:31:47.273
maybe I'm over the halfway mark and

00:31:47.273 --> 00:31:51.932
more Toward an extrovert leader.

00:31:51.932 --> 00:31:58.490
I'm an introvert as a person, and I had to
come and I had to learn that about myself.

00:31:58.490 --> 00:32:04.680
I didn't really know that when I
started in a leadership position.

00:32:04.680 --> 00:32:07.774
When I went from general counsel to COO,

00:32:07.774 --> 00:32:12.660
that was a really hard transition for
me, as a person.

00:32:12.660 --> 00:32:17.510
Because as a lawyer you can stay in
a room and research and write and

00:32:17.510 --> 00:32:22.400
do all the things you have to do alone for
a long time.

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:27.312
And now probably the only time you have
to talk to people is when you have

00:32:27.312 --> 00:32:31.639
to deal with other lawyers or
have to present to the client, so

00:32:31.639 --> 00:32:35.820
in a way it's a kind of
introverted profession, it can be.

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:40.806
And so, when I became COO and
had to really be a leader and

00:32:40.806 --> 00:32:46.112
I had my own management team,
it took me a while to adjust and

00:32:46.112 --> 00:32:48.979
to be comfortable in that role,

00:32:48.979 --> 00:32:54.130
even sitting at the head of
the table was a transition.

00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:59.260
I just wasn't used to it, I was used to
being that person behind the scenes who

00:32:59.260 --> 00:33:04.690
worked until three o'clock in the morning
if I had to, and find the right answer.

00:33:04.690 --> 00:33:09.994
But I wasn't used to sitting at
the table making decisions quickly and

00:33:09.994 --> 00:33:12.870
sharing a vision.

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:17.975
And even going from COO to CEO was
different because when you're COO no one

00:33:17.975 --> 00:33:23.355
asks you what your vision is, because
as COO you're supposed to carry out

00:33:23.355 --> 00:33:28.575
the vision of the CEO, so
I've had different transition points.

00:33:28.575 --> 00:33:32.062
So going from general accounts or the COO,

00:33:32.062 --> 00:33:35.472
I had to learn to be more of the leader,
I had to learn to make decisions.

00:33:35.472 --> 00:33:37.142
I had to learn how to manage,

00:33:37.142 --> 00:33:40.732
and that's not something they
teach you at law school.

00:33:40.732 --> 00:33:45.154
I hope they teach it at business school
[LAUGH], but I didn't go to business, so

00:33:45.154 --> 00:33:46.032
I don't know.

00:33:46.032 --> 00:33:49.224
But, so I had to do it on the job, and

00:33:49.224 --> 00:33:55.184
I didn't have the luxury of being
able to research for two weeks and

00:33:55.184 --> 00:34:00.790
get back to executives who
wanted to answer on the spot.

00:34:00.790 --> 00:34:05.616
And the executives that were part of my
first team were my peers before I became

00:34:05.616 --> 00:34:10.293
COO, except a couple of them weren't
really that invested in my success,

00:34:10.293 --> 00:34:12.685
I think that's the nice way to put it.

00:34:12.685 --> 00:34:15.745
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so, it took me a while to make

00:34:15.745 --> 00:34:19.004
the tough decisions that some
of them would have to leave and

00:34:19.004 --> 00:34:22.866
some of them made that decision on
their own, but that was a hard time.

00:34:22.866 --> 00:34:27.895
I mean it really took me, to be honest,
about six years to get my own team in

00:34:27.895 --> 00:34:33.254
place, and that's way too long,
that it should've happened much quicker,

00:34:33.254 --> 00:34:37.500
but they were my peers,
the founder was still at the company.

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:42.795
So, even though, I was running the
company, I had someone who could veto me,

00:34:42.795 --> 00:34:46.164
and someone that they could
go around me to get to,

00:34:46.164 --> 00:34:51.291
to get the decision if they wanted,
if they didn't like my decision.

00:34:51.291 --> 00:34:54.418
So, then when I became CEO I had to
start thinking about the vision thing,

00:34:54.418 --> 00:34:57.550
because then all of a sudden people
are like, what is your vision?

00:34:57.550 --> 00:35:00.952
It's like, I don't know,
I've been COO for ten years,

00:35:00.952 --> 00:35:05.066
I've never had to have a vision before,
I jus did what the CEO want it.

00:35:05.066 --> 00:35:08.661
So that took me a while
to really focus on that,

00:35:08.661 --> 00:35:13.920
I wanted to do original programming,
I wanted to have a new brand and

00:35:13.920 --> 00:35:19.939
have something that was really had
the values that I believed in personally.

00:35:19.939 --> 00:35:24.970
And that was tough because all of a sudden
I really didn't have anyone to talk to.

00:35:24.970 --> 00:35:29.950
When you're COO you can always,
you do all the work and

00:35:29.950 --> 00:35:33.640
you get all the blame [LAUGH], it's
something doesn't go bad, cowrite, but

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:36.210
you have someone to bounce ideas off of.

00:35:36.210 --> 00:35:40.800
So, when Bob Johnson left as CEO,
I remember the first

00:35:40.800 --> 00:35:45.790
decision I had to make was
the decision whether to

00:35:45.790 --> 00:35:51.040
carry Rosa Parks' memorial service on BET.

00:35:52.410 --> 00:35:57.390
And we had not had a habit of doing
funerals or memorial service.

00:35:57.390 --> 00:36:02.548
I think Rosa Parks was the first
civil rights leader that had died,

00:36:02.548 --> 00:36:08.549
where it was really, since I was at
the network, that was really a big thing.

00:36:08.549 --> 00:36:15.305
But I knew that funerals in the black
community could last many hours.

00:36:15.305 --> 00:36:20.461
[LAUGH] And
then there was no definite into it.

00:36:20.461 --> 00:36:25.393
I knew that it wasn't going to be
a ratings driver, there were going to be

00:36:25.393 --> 00:36:30.166
some people that watched it but
people weren't going to flop to it.

00:36:30.166 --> 00:36:35.012
But I also knew as one of the few, I think
at this time we had some competition,

00:36:35.012 --> 00:36:37.101
as one of the few black networks,

00:36:37.101 --> 00:36:41.374
if we didn't cover it we were going to
be subject to a lot of criticism.

00:36:41.374 --> 00:36:45.240
because people would say, how could
you not cover Rosa Parks' funeral?

00:36:45.240 --> 00:36:46.543
It's Rosa Parks.

00:36:46.543 --> 00:36:49.094
So,I remember trying to
make that decision, and

00:36:49.094 --> 00:36:52.810
I remember I was in the back
of a car going to the airport.

00:36:52.810 --> 00:36:55.082
And I was like, well,
Deborah, what do you think?

00:36:55.082 --> 00:36:57.870
[LAUGH] Well, I don't know Deborah,
what do you think?

00:36:57.870 --> 00:37:04.077
I mean, it was the first time that
I literally had no one to talk to.

00:37:04.077 --> 00:37:08.056
And I remember just that odd feeling that,
okay,

00:37:08.056 --> 00:37:11.780
now I'm CEO,
the buck really stops with me.

00:37:11.780 --> 00:37:16.530
I have no one else to blame,
no one else to say, well, he vetoed me, or

00:37:16.530 --> 00:37:20.400
it was really his decision that
I really had to stand up for

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:24.950
the decisions I made, and
be the leader of the company.

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:31.330
And the leader of BET,
because of who we are as a network,

00:37:31.330 --> 00:37:33.900
is much more than just
leading the company.

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:38.028
It's also being a leader in the community.

00:37:38.028 --> 00:37:41.302
And taking positions on policy issues,

00:37:41.302 --> 00:37:45.348
having relationships
with the administration,

00:37:45.348 --> 00:37:50.175
making decisions on how we
cover administrations and not.

00:37:51.235 --> 00:37:54.499
But to answer the question,
in case you're wondering,

00:37:54.499 --> 00:37:56.547
I decided not to carry the funeral.

00:37:56.547 --> 00:37:59.990
We did get some criticism for it.

00:37:59.990 --> 00:38:02.220
Now, we have BET.com.

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:09.831
So when there are funerals, like when
Maya Angelou died, we cover it on BET.com.

00:38:09.831 --> 00:38:14.640
And we also have another network called
Centric, and sometimes we cover it there.

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:19.060
But we try not to devote three or
four hours

00:38:19.060 --> 00:38:22.830
on the main network to a funeral that
people are probably not going to watch.

00:38:22.830 --> 00:38:26.600
But people do say, well,
CNN covered it, why didn't BET?

00:38:26.600 --> 00:38:29.274
Well, we're not a 24-hour news network.

00:38:29.274 --> 00:38:32.823
&gt;&gt; Yep.
So it's fine to make decisions like that,

00:38:32.823 --> 00:38:36.190
but it's also a lot of responsibility.

00:38:36.190 --> 00:38:40.660
And back to your question about
what people think of the network.

00:38:40.660 --> 00:38:44.980
I remember once as we tried to
deal with this issue of videos,

00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:49.894
the good news is we don't have any
videos on air anymore mainly because you

00:38:49.894 --> 00:38:54.807
get them online, so that took the issue
away, but when we had a couple

00:38:54.807 --> 00:38:59.805
of video shows still on the air and
we were getting all this criticism,

00:38:59.805 --> 00:39:04.888
I had a standards committee of
executives who would look at the videos,

00:39:04.888 --> 00:39:08.616
send the videos back to
the label if they had issues,

00:39:08.616 --> 00:39:12.559
ask them to edit it, and
it was a give and take process.

00:39:12.559 --> 00:39:16.400
And then, if they couldn't come to
a decision, they would bring video to me.

00:39:16.400 --> 00:39:19.752
So, if the video got to
me it was a bad video.

00:39:19.752 --> 00:39:23.233
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] But probably by big celebrity.

00:39:23.233 --> 00:39:27.682
Of course, the First Amendment comes
into it, the question of, do you want to

00:39:27.682 --> 00:39:32.070
silence some voices, and not other voices,
and where do you draw the line?

00:39:32.070 --> 00:39:37.365
And I had all those thoughts
when I was trying to decide our

00:39:37.365 --> 00:39:42.439
position on videos, and
language and visuals, and so

00:39:42.439 --> 00:39:50.300
that's the way that I relate it back to
it, because I think it's very similar.

00:39:50.300 --> 00:39:53.070
Those of you who are in law school.

00:39:53.070 --> 00:39:55.900
You do have First Amendment rights.

00:39:55.900 --> 00:39:58.190
But you can't yell fire in a theatre.

00:39:58.190 --> 00:40:00.760
That's a safety issue.

00:40:00.760 --> 00:40:07.620
And that's a Freedom of speech
argument in its purest sense.

00:40:07.620 --> 00:40:13.740
But when you're talking about a platform,
I think you can draw the lines.

00:40:13.740 --> 00:40:17.180
At a certain point,
you still have to service your audience.

00:40:17.180 --> 00:40:23.160
And you want people to feel like you
respect both views, or all views.

00:40:23.160 --> 00:40:26.320
But on the other hand, I think
there's a line you can draw when you

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:30.290
just said, not on my platform.

00:40:30.290 --> 00:40:32.739
&gt;&gt; And now, speaking [INAUDIBLE] audience.

00:40:32.739 --> 00:40:35.750
Final question from me
before we move to a Q&amp;A.

00:40:35.750 --> 00:40:37.300
It's the big question, Debra.

00:40:37.300 --> 00:40:38.350
&gt;&gt; Okay, the big one.

00:40:38.350 --> 00:40:39.020
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's the big one.

00:40:40.480 --> 00:40:44.150
Talk us through President Obama's
farewell party.

00:40:44.150 --> 00:40:44.790
&gt;&gt; Mm.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:44.790 --> 00:40:47.110
&gt;&gt; What was it like?

00:40:47.110 --> 00:40:50.736
What would we be most
surprised to find out about?

00:40:50.736 --> 00:40:51.608
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:40:51.608 --> 00:40:54.990
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Let me think.

00:40:54.990 --> 00:40:56.680
That's a great question.

00:40:56.680 --> 00:41:04.300
Well first of all, it was the most amazing
experience of my career and my life.

00:41:04.300 --> 00:41:08.830
To be able to go into the White House and

00:41:08.830 --> 00:41:13.520
produce a concert in tribute to
the first black president and

00:41:13.520 --> 00:41:20.200
his wife was something I never
thought I would be able to experience.

00:41:20.200 --> 00:41:24.840
And just to give you some background,
the Idea for the concert came up.

00:41:24.840 --> 00:41:28.555
I was at a brunch at,
I think I can say this.

00:41:28.555 --> 00:41:29.625
[LAUGH].
Valerie.

00:41:29.625 --> 00:41:30.205
[LAUGH].

00:41:30.205 --> 00:41:33.510
Gee, I'm looking at Stephanie,
who used to work at the White House.

00:41:33.510 --> 00:41:36.550
I was at brunch at
Valerie Jarrett's house.

00:41:36.550 --> 00:41:38.560
And there were maybe ten women there.

00:41:38.560 --> 00:41:40.670
Friends of the first lady.

00:41:40.670 --> 00:41:43.942
And it was the first time I had
actually sat down over a meal.

00:41:43.942 --> 00:41:45.650
Maybe it's was the second time.

00:41:45.650 --> 00:41:48.330
Because I had lunch with here once
when she first moved to town.

00:41:48.330 --> 00:41:49.210
Just the two of us.

00:41:49.210 --> 00:41:52.030
But anyway, I was invited to this brunch.

00:41:52.030 --> 00:41:56.630
And we're sitting down,
talking about things.And

00:41:56.630 --> 00:41:59.402
Michelle Obama just said,
off the top of her head.

00:41:59.402 --> 00:42:04.340
It would be really great if BET could
do a concert at the White House.

00:42:04.340 --> 00:42:08.292
And I was like, yeah.

00:42:08.292 --> 00:42:09.580
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:42:09.580 --> 00:42:10.340
&gt;&gt; It's one of those

00:42:10.340 --> 00:42:12.490
things where you try not to be too exited.

00:42:12.490 --> 00:42:15.700
&gt;&gt; I was like, yeah,
that would be really great.

00:42:15.700 --> 00:42:18.268
I said, when would you want to do it?

00:42:18.268 --> 00:42:19.035
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:42:19.035 --> 00:42:20.340
We're pretty free.

00:42:20.340 --> 00:42:21.130
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:42:21.130 --> 00:42:22.274
And so she said, yeah.

00:42:22.274 --> 00:42:24.640
And we can have all our favorite artists.

00:42:24.640 --> 00:42:26.080
I'm like, yeah.

00:42:26.080 --> 00:42:27.470
And so we started talking about it.

00:42:27.470 --> 00:42:29.500
And this was maybe three years ago.

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:31.030
It was a long time ago.

00:42:31.030 --> 00:42:34.380
&gt;&gt; And so she said, well, I'm going
to have my people call your people.

00:42:34.380 --> 00:42:35.190
I'm like, yeah.

00:42:35.190 --> 00:42:36.800
We're going to really do this.

00:42:36.800 --> 00:42:37.940
I never thought it would happen.

00:42:37.940 --> 00:42:40.240
I thought we were just talking.

00:42:40.240 --> 00:42:44.670
But we started, our teams got together.

00:42:44.670 --> 00:42:49.404
The problem was, PBS had the rights to
do all concerts in the White House.

00:42:49.404 --> 00:42:50.510
Right.

00:42:50.510 --> 00:42:54.850
So they tried to partner us with PBS.

00:42:54.850 --> 00:42:56.070
That didn't work.

00:42:56.070 --> 00:42:59.500
And so
it kind of got put on the back burner.

00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:04.850
And then, it ended up where we found
a way to do it by ourselves without PBS.

00:43:04.850 --> 00:43:11.600
And it ended up being the last
concert of that administration.

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:15.200
And by then,
it just took on a whole different meaning.

00:43:15.200 --> 00:43:17.210
It became a tribute concert.

00:43:17.210 --> 00:43:22.770
So I think one thing you would be
surprised about is that the president and

00:43:22.770 --> 00:43:27.520
first lady actually had a lot
of input into who performed.

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:28.180
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:43:28.180 --> 00:43:31.570
&gt;&gt; They just said, this is our ideal list.

00:43:31.570 --> 00:43:33.150
These are our favorite artists.

00:43:33.150 --> 00:43:34.780
And this is who we want to perform.

00:43:34.780 --> 00:43:38.120
And we want De La Soul,
and blah blah blah.

00:43:38.120 --> 00:43:40.400
So that whole process was amazing.

00:43:40.400 --> 00:43:42.850
The back and forth with the White House.

00:43:42.850 --> 00:43:45.410
&gt;&gt; Did they get on the dance floor,
and get involved?

00:43:45.410 --> 00:43:47.750
There was not a dance floor,
but they did dance.

00:43:47.750 --> 00:43:50.088
At the concert, they danced a lot.

00:43:50.088 --> 00:43:53.440
If you watched it,
you would see that on TV.

00:43:53.440 --> 00:43:58.020
And I had the most incredible experience
of sitting next to Michelle Obama.

00:43:58.020 --> 00:44:02.072
There are just so many parts of that
concert that were just unbelievable.

00:44:02.072 --> 00:44:07.740
The other great part of it
was it did become a tribute.

00:44:07.740 --> 00:44:12.590
And the president and first lady were
surprised that it was a tribute.

00:44:12.590 --> 00:44:16.640
So when the performers started paying
tribute to them from the stage.

00:44:16.640 --> 00:44:19.060
They both kept looking at me saying,
we didn't expect this.

00:44:19.060 --> 00:44:19.940
We didn't expect this.

00:44:19.940 --> 00:44:21.210
This is really something.

00:44:21.210 --> 00:44:24.330
It's like, how could we come in here,
the last concert, and

00:44:24.330 --> 00:44:26.060
not make it a tribute concert?

00:44:26.060 --> 00:44:31.215
So it was really,
it was pretty incredible.

00:44:31.215 --> 00:44:38.550
And it was fun seeing who they wanted and
who they cared about.

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:42.118
I can say, it was a few problems
getting some people in.

00:44:42.118 --> 00:44:46.153
Because there are things you have
to do to get into the White House.

00:44:46.153 --> 00:44:49.236
Sure.
[LAUGH] And things you cannot have done.

00:44:49.236 --> 00:44:51.087
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:44:51.087 --> 00:44:53.450
More importantly, yeah.

00:44:53.450 --> 00:44:56.620
&gt;&gt; So
that was a little bit of a balancing act.

00:44:56.620 --> 00:44:57.569
And I won't name names.

00:44:57.569 --> 00:44:59.765
Sure.
But it was a funny process.

00:44:59.765 --> 00:45:02.000
[LAUGHS]
&gt;&gt; I'll let the audience ask for

00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:02.755
the names.

00:45:02.755 --> 00:45:04.765
&gt;&gt; Right.
But it was a lot of fun.

00:45:04.765 --> 00:45:07.270
Perfect.
And we had a party afterwards.

00:45:07.270 --> 00:45:11.770
And then Dave Chappelle talked
about it on Saturday Night Live.

00:45:11.770 --> 00:45:13.800
How cool was that?

00:45:13.800 --> 00:45:16.518
He was like,
I was at this night at the White House.

00:45:16.518 --> 00:45:17.369
And we're like,

00:45:17.369 --> 00:45:20.410
wow, he's talking about our party
&gt;&gt; That never happens

00:45:20.410 --> 00:45:22.222
with our parties here.

00:45:22.222 --> 00:45:23.318
No?
It doesn't?

00:45:23.318 --> 00:45:24.262
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:45:24.262 --> 00:45:27.720
you may have a chance to recruit
some of the Obama family.

00:45:27.720 --> 00:45:29.787
You can help us [INAUDIBLE], yeah.

00:45:29.787 --> 00:45:34.690
Yeah, in the next couple of years.

